Falls out of the work on k45 (CVE-2026-85783) and has to be decided BEFORE
scrypt is built, not after.

The stanza cap from k45 bounds the NUMBER of stanzas. For X25519 that is
enough, because every stanza costs a constant amount (one scalar
multiplication, ~2.7 ms after the hoisting). For scrypt it is not: there the
work factor is a decimal number carried IN the header and executed by the
reader before anything is authenticated -- exactly the property that made k45 a
CVE, except that this time the attacker picks the per-stanza cost freely
instead of having to buy it 98 bytes at a time.

A cap of 128 stanzas multiplied by an arbitrary work factor is not a cap.

TO DO before scrypt is built
1. Read c2sp.org/age, the scrypt recipient type section: the spec constrains
   what a reader may do with the work factor. Read it first, then decide --
   not the other way round.
2. Measure what age 1.2.1 and rage 0.12.1 actually accept as an upper bound
   (empirically, the way k45 was measured), so that a limit here does not
   reject a file the reference implementations write.
3. Decide whether the limit becomes configurable the way max_stanzas is.
4. Also: the spec requires an scrypt stanza to be the ONLY stanza in the
   header. That removes the identities x stanzas multiplication -- check
   whether it changes the arithmetic.

Context: k43 records that scrypt is to come from CryptX 0.088
(Crypt::KeyDerivation::scrypt_pbkdf) without adding a dependency.
The CPANSec report behind k45 arrived 2026-09-04.

MAINTAINER DECISION 2026-09-09: max_work_factor, default 22, configurable
exactly like max_stanzas in lib/Crypt/Age/Header.pm.

No code lands now. There is no scrypt parser to guard -- lib/Crypt/Age/Stanza/
holds X25519.pm only -- so an attribute today would be speculative. This
ticket records the requirement; the cap is built together with the recipient
type.

RESULTS FOR THE FOUR TODO POINTS

1. Spec (c2sp.org/age, scrypt recipient type). On the work factor the spec
   only SHOULDs: "the identity implementation SHOULD apply an upper limit",
   no number. What it MUSTs instead, and what the cap has to be built
   alongside:
     * stanza body is exactly 32 bytes, checked BEFORE any decryption attempt
     * work factor is decimal, no leading zero: ^[1-9][0-9]*$
     * an scrypt stanza must be the ONLY stanza in the header

2. Upper bound of the reference implementations.
   READ in the age source (not measured here): maxWorkFactor defaults to 22,
   SetMaxWorkFactor allows at most 30; age writes 18.
   VERIFIED in the vendored testkit: vector scrypt_work_factor_23 expects
   "header failure", comment "work factor is very high, would take a long time
   to compute". So a limit above 22 breaks the testkit, and 22 accepts
   everything age writes itself. The plain "scrypt" vector uses 10.
   STILL OPEN: rage 0.12.1 -- what it writes by default and whether it reads
   22. Measurement in flight, result appended separately.

3. Configurable: yes, see the decision above.

4. "Only stanza" confirmed as a MUST by the spec and by two vectors that both
   expect "header failure": scrypt_and_x25519 and scrypt_double. That removes
   the identities x stanzas multiplication for scrypt: exactly one scrypt
   invocation per file, bounded by the work factor alone. The k45 stanza cap
   therefore does not bound scrypt at all -- max_work_factor is the only thing
   that does, which is why it is a precondition and not a follow-up.

REQUIREMENTS THIS TICKET WAS MISSING
The regex and the 32-byte body above are MUSTs with their own vectors, not
niceties. They belong in the acceptance list next to the limit.

